  "summary": "Magnificent Islamic architectural style developed by the Timurid Empire in Persia and Central Asia.",
  "description": "Known for its grand scale, intricate tilework with Persian motifs, high portals, and bulbous domes. Created to showcase the power and sophistication of Timur (Tamerlane) and his successors, blending Persian, Mongol, and Islamic elements.",
  "culturalContext": "The Timurid style (1370-1507) — Tamerlane's imperial building program at Samarkand and Herat — fused Persian, Turkic and Central Asian craft into the most monumental Islamic architecture of its age: giant ribbed domes of double curvature, monumental pishtaq portals covered in polychrome tile mosaic, muqarnas half-domes and the Gur-e Amir's fluted turquoise drum. The Timurid Renaissance also exported its artists to Mughal India, seeding the Taj Mahal's lineage.",
